N2  - Background: Pain and anxiety remain major concerns during transrectal ultrasound (TRUS)-guided prostate biopsy despite the use of periprostatic nerve block (PPNB). AcuTENS, a form of transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation applied over acupuncture points, may enhance multimodal analgesia and anxiolysis. Methods: A double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial was conducted at the University Malaya Medical Center to evaluate AcuTENS as an adjunct to PPNB during TRUS-guided biopsy. The intervention group received PPNB with AcuTENS, whereas the placebo group received PPNB with sham stimulation. Pain was assessed using a 0–10 visual analog scale (VAS) during probe insertion, lignocaine injection, and biopsy. State anxiety (STAI-S) was measured pre-and post-procedure. Statistical comparisons were performed using independent and paired-samples t-tests. Results: Fifty-three men were included in this study (mean age 72.5 ± 7.6 years), with 28 in the intervention group and 25 in the placebo arm. The intervention group reported significantly lower overall and procedural pain scores compared with placebo during PPNB injection (p = 0.010) and biopsy (p = 0.003), but not during probe insertion (p = 0.65). Mean overall pain scores were 2.71 ± 1.30 versus 3.92 ± 1.26 (p = 0.001). Anxiety (STAI-S) significantly decreased post-procedure in the intervention group (p < 0.001), but remained unchanged in the placebo group (p = 0.538). No AcuTENS-related complications were observed. Conclusions: When combined with standard PPNB, AcuTENS significantly reduced pain and situational anxiety during TRUS-guided prostate biopsy and was well tolerated. As a safe, noninvasive, and easily applied adjunct, AcuTENS shows promise for integration into routine outpatient biopsy protocols.
