N2  - Study Objectives: A three-year grant from the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA), supported a program to deploy training and facilitated bedside utilization of various medications and procedures to address pain management without the use of opioids. This Alternatives of Opioids (ALTO) program, instituted within a busy level 1 trauma center emergency department (ED), provided electronic medical record (EMR) based order sets and enhanced training and bedside support for various procedures including, acupuncture, trigger point injections, regional nerve blocks (RNBs), and Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation (TENS) to provide alternatives to opioids to treat pain. Methods: This is a single site prospective cohort study assessed all adult (18yrs or older) patients with reported pain, as stratified by organ system comparing the 6 months prior to program initiation against the full-time course of the program (8/1/2021- 11/30/2024). This program was initiated within the only regional level 1 (∼95K adult ED patients/year) trauma center ED located in Greenville, SC. We assessed overall utilization of the various alternative treatments and tracked overall percentage of patients that received any opioid stratified by organ system while in the ED. Results: Non-opioid pharmacotherapeutics were most utilized ALTO therapy (85% of enrolled patients), followed by TENS therapy (72%), RNBs (14%), acupuncture (<1%), and TPIs (<1%), respectively. Commonly used pharmacotherapeutics included non-steroidal anti-inflammatories (NSAIDS) and steroidal agents, acetaminophen, skeletal muscle relaxants, benzodiazepines, antiemetics, anticonvulsants such as gabapentin, and topical agents such as lidocaine patches. As shown in the Table, we tracked trends in opioid utilization within the ED as stratified by body organ system. Decreases were seen across several organ systems including Headache (0.9% decrease or about 162 patients spared opioids), Chest (1.4% decrease or about 545 patients spared opioids), Abdominal (0.7% decrease or about 730 patients spared opioids), and Extremities (1.5% decrease or 1020 patients spared opioids). Conclusion: We report decreases across several organ systems within this level 1 trauma center ED. These decreases spared any opioid use in a total of ∼2500 patients. This data is limited in that it includes all patients with pain in the ED within these time periods and includes use of any opioid. This data does not assess the individual MMEs per patient, it reports on overall opioid usage in the ED. Future goals include ongoing assessment of opioid utilization after cessation of this program. [Formula presented] No, authors do not have interests to disclose
