N2  - Background: Abdominal pain in chronic pancreatitis (CP) is common, difficult to treat, and profoundly impairs quality of life. TEA is a noninvasive, needleless acupuncture method, that uses a small wearable device to self-administer weak electrical current via surface electrodes placed at selected acupoints and has shown to reduce abdominal pain in different GI disorders. This study aims to assess the feasibility and analgesic effect of TEA in patients with painful CP. Methods: This was a single-center, single-arm, open-label, clinical trial. Adults with CP based on Mayo Criteria and with abdominal pain at least once a week were eligible. The study involved 3 periods: run-in (2 weeks), TEA intervention (4 weeks), and observation (4 weeks). Enrolled subjects completed daily pain diaries, weekly assessments of brief pain inventory (BPI), and pre- and post- treatment evaluation of COMPAT-SF, PROMIS-29, and HADS. During the run-in period, subjects without pain ≥4/10 on visual analogue scale for at least 5/14 days were excluded. TEA was self-administered at home over two daily treatment sessions of 30 minutes each, using acupoint ST36 (leg) and parameter P100 (100Hz, 0.5ms, 0.1s-on and 0.4s-off, and adjustable amplitude). Outcomes included: A) enrollment feasibility; B) analgesic effect; C) patient global impression of change; and D) treatment acceptability. Analgesic effect was analyzed comparing baseline and end of treatment measurements. Results: Between October 2023 and July 2024, 164 patients with CP were approached, of which 12 subjects were enrolled. Reasons for exclusion included not enough pain (17%), prior or awaiting surgery (12%), chronic opioid use (12%), current endotherapy (6%), lack of interest (38%), and other (15%). Two subjects were excluded after run-in period. A total of 10 subjects received the TEA device. One subject withdrew before starting TEA due to unrelated pneumonia. Two subjects did not complete sufficient assessments. Of 7 patients with complete case analysis, there was nonsignificant change between pre- and post-TEA on maximal daily pain score (p=0.27), average daily pain score (p=0.12), BPI pain score (p=0.27), BPI interference score (n=0.66), COMPAT-SF (p=0.4), PROMISPr (p=0.9), HADS anxiety score (p=0.33), and HADS depression score (p=0.41, Table 1). Although non-significant, 71% of patients had reduction of BPI pain score and 57% had lower BPI interference score. Of 9 patients who received TEA treatments, 44% reported global improvement of abdominal pain, 67% liked the intervention, and 56% would recommend TEA to others (Table 2). No adverse events were found. Conclusion: TEA is a feasible analgesic intervention for painful CP. A larger RCT assessing the efficacy of TEA in CP is on the way. If effective, TEA may be an alternative therapy with better safety profile to other interventions used for painful CP.
