N2  - Background/Aims: To explore the integrative effects and mechanisms of transcutaneous electrical acustimulation (TEA) on postoperative recovery after endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP). Materials and Methods: A total of 62 patients scheduled for the ERCP procedure were randomized to receive TEA (n=31) at acupoints PC6 and ST36 or Sham-TEA (n=31) at sham points starting from one day before ERCP (Day 0) to the second day after ERCP (post-ERCP Day 2, PED 2). TEA or Sham-TEA was performed for 30 min, twice daily with the pulse frequency of 100 Hz for PC6 and frequency of 25Hz for ST36. Abdominal pain scores, autonomic functions via the spectral analysis of heart rate variability (HRV), post-ERCP hospital days and incidence of post-ERCP pancreatitis (PEP) and hyperamylasemia (PEH) were recorded; meanwhile, circulatory levels of motilin, ghrelin, cholecystokinin (CCK), TNF-α were assessed by ELISA before and after the TEA/sham-TEA treatment. Results: 1) TEA, but not Sham-TEA, decreased post-ERCP abdominal pain score (PED 2 vs. Day 0: 0[0, 2] vs. 2[0, 3], P<0.05 on TEA group; 3(2,3) vs. 2(0,3), P>0.05 on Sham-TEA group) and shortened the post-ERCP hospital day of 16.7% (P<0.05 vs. Sham-TEA group); 2) The incidence of PEP and PEH in TEA group was slightly lower than Sham-TEA group, but showed no significance (9.7%[3/31] vs. 19.4[6/31], P=0.236); 3) Both TEA and Sham-TEA group revealed elevated serum TNF-α and reduced plasma ghrelin (both P<0.05) after ERCP (PED2) in comparison with those before ERCP (Day 0). However, compared with Sham-TEA, TEA resulted in significant lower TNF-α level and higher ghrelin level after ERCP (TEA group vs. Sham-TEA group on PED2: 1.67±0.48 pg/mL vs. 2.18±0.75 pg/mL for TNF-α; 1.75±0.70 ng/mL vs. 1.17±0.60 ng/mL for ghrelin, both P<0.05 on PED2, but both P>0.05 on Day 0). No significant difference of motilin or CCK were recorded between TEA group and Sham-TEA group (both P>0.05, see Figure); 4) Compared with Sham-TEA, TEA decreased sympathetic activity by 21.8% and increased vagal activity by 28.3% (both P<0.05) on PED 2. Conclusions: TEA at PC6 and ST36 during the perioperative period of ERCP reduces abdominal pain and plasma TNF-α but enhances vagal activity. These findings suggest that the analgesic effect of TEA might be medicated via the cholinergic anti-inflammatory pathway. [figure presented]
