N2  - Background and Objectives: With the increased recognition of the opioid epidemic, many patients and providers seek safer alternatives for effective pain management in the emergency department (ED). Emergency Department Alternatives to Opioids (ALTO) programs utilize nonopioid medications and procedures to treat pain in the ED with the aim of minimizing opioid use. This study sought to evaluate ALTO treatment and opioid use before and after implementation of the ALTO program and report patient-reported outcomes for patients receiving ALTO procedures. Methods: The ALTO program was implemented in our Level 1 Trauma Center ED on August 1, 2021. We obtained electronic health record data for all patients presenting to the ED in the 12 months (August 1, 2020-July 31, 2021) prior to and 28 months (August 1, 2021-November 30, 2023) following implementation. Opioid use and nonopioid medication and ALTO procedure use were compared before and after implementation. ALTO procedures include transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS), acupuncture, trigger point injections (TPI), and regional nerve blocks (RNB). Additionally, we report patient-reported satisfaction with care and pain relief in the ED for patients treated with ALTO procedures following implementation. Results: There were 78,962 encounters in the 12-month pre-implementation period and 187,629 encounters in the 28-month post-implementation period. Following program implementation, ALTO procedure use increased from 98 procedures performed (TENS = 0, acupuncture = 0, TPI = 12, RNB = 86) to 612 performed (TENS = 279, acupuncture = 54, TPI = 103, RNB = 235). Additionally, nonopioid analgesic medication use increased from 33.7% to 35.0% (p < 0.01), and opioid use decreased from 18.0% to 17.3% (p < 0.01) following implementation. Complete survey data was available for 234 (38%) patients treated with ALTO procedures post-implementation. Among these patients, the median patient satisfaction score was 9 out of 10 (IQR: 6-10); 85% experienced pain relief from the ALTO procedure, and 89% would use the ALTO procedure again for future pain relief. Conclusion: Opioid use decreased, and nonopioid medication and ALTO procedure use increased following implementation of the ALTO program within our ED. Patients who received ALTO procedures report high patient satisfaction, pain relief, and willingness to use the ALTO procedure again for future pain.
