N2  - Background and Aims: COPD is a chronic respiratory disease characterized by progressive airflow limitation. The commonly reported exertional dyspnoea is one recurrent symptom that limits level of physical activity. Acu-TENS has been reported to reduce level of dyspnoea and to increase exercise capacity in people with COPD. Yet, underlying mechanism remains unknown. This study aims to examine effect of Acu-TENS on cigarette smoke-induced COPD-like animal model and explore its associated mechanisms. Methods: Forty SD rats were randomly allocated into cigarette-smoking(CS) or sham air(SA) exposure group, further divided into Acu-TENS groups (CS-AT/SA-AT) or control groups (CS-C/SA-C). All rats were exposed to CS (CS-AT/CS-C) or SA (SA-AT/SA-C) for 1 hour/session, 2 sessions/day, consecutively for 56 days. Before exposure, they received AT or control for 45 min. Non-invasive outcome measures such as breathing frequency(f) and tidal volume(TV) were measured before and after 45-minute AT/control session by double-chamber plethysmography. On 57th day, all animals were anesthetized to assess lung function (FVC, FEV, FEF), respiratory compliance(Crs) and airway resistance(sRaw) by flexivent. Morphology of lung tissues was assessed by mean linear intercept(MLI). Results: Compared with SA-C group, CS-C group demonstrated significantly higher levels of sRaw (p = 0.014), Crs (p < 0.001) and increased alveolar spacing (MLI, p < 0.001) accompanied by reduced volume of FEV0.05 (p = 0.045). When examining interaction effect of CS exposure and AT treatment, significant interaction effect was found in Crs (p = 0.006), forced expiratory flow (FEF0.2, p = 0.003) and sRaw (p = 0.044) while no difference was detected in MLI and other parameters. Conclusion: The findings suggested that Acu-TENS alleviated airway resistance thereby increasing expiratory flow and potentially expiratory volume.
