N2  - Although acupunctiue has excellent clinical results many patients reflise such treatment clue to rejection of the idea of needles being introduced in their bodies. Recently electronic stimulation was described to obtain same effect as with needle acupuncture. Throughout the years many studies have been published explaining pathophysiology of acupimctiu e effects. Among the many factors there is an association of acupuncture and results regarding Endorphin. Dynoiphin and Serotonin. Our study was done with 6 patients (3 male, 3 female) who were diagnosed with stroke sequelae (1-69.4). Parkinson (G-20). [senility (R54) and Presbycusis (HSU.9). Transcutaneous weekly stimulation was perfonned on each patient once a week. Pictures were taken just prior and after treatment so that telomere, asbestos, acetylcholine, serotonin, and dopamine could be BDORT evaluated on the printed images Conclusion: telomere, acetylcholine, serotonin and dopamine increased significantly when compared to measurements done just prior to treatment but decreased when measured prior to treatment a week later for all patients. Asbestos decreased and maintained low levels up to the next visit. Patients referred better balance, ambulation and auditive conditions. They showed cognitive impiovement and less fatigue. Due to encouraging positive results fiitiue studies regarding other parameters will be earned out by us.
