N2  - Introduction: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network guideline mentions considering traditional Chinese medicine (TCM) for breast cancer without specific guidelines. Many systematic reviews (SR) focus on different modalities of TCM on different stages of breast cancer and their symptoms and side effects. We aimed to summarize all high level evidence for TCM and breast cancer to provide evidence for clinical practice. Methods: Literature searches were conducted in Pubmed, Embase, Scopus, Cochrane Library and Epistemonikos from their inception to January 2019 to identify SR and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials (RCT) published in English. Patients with breast cancer (any stage) were included. TCM modalities included all kinds of pharmaceutical and non-pharmaceutical therapies. AMSTAR-2 and ROBIS were used to evaluate methodological quality of SRs. Results: 26 SRs and 1 Cochrane review including 340 RCTs with 24,082 participants were reviewed, which were published in English between 2006-2018. Eight SRs involved 7371 breast cancer patients who suffered from side effects due to hormone therapy and chemotherapy, these patients were treated by acupoint stimulation (manual, electric, acupoint injection and self-acupressure) or massage compared with placebo or conventional therapy. Eight SRs included 5836 patients with hot flushes treated by acupuncture or electroacupuncture and compared with sham acupuncture or conventional therapy. Seven SRs focused on 9498 patients receiving using conventional therapy combined with herbal medicine, acupuncture or massage. Three SRs involved 1040 patients with side effects/symptoms treated with acupuncture or electroacupuncture or acupressure compared with sham acupuncture or conventional therapy. One SR involved 3142 breast cancer patients treated after mastectomy with herbal medicine compared with conventional therapy. Outcome measures included; symptoms, quality-of-life, long-term survival, and adverse events. The quality of 27 SRs was low to moderate evaluated by ROBIS. Four SRs reported potential bias of included RCTs and one SR reported conflict of interest of included RCTs evaluated by AMSTAR-2. Conclusions: Current evidence focuses mainly on acupoint stimulation for breast cancer side-effects with moderate quality. Future studies should build robust evidence on the effectiveness and safety of herbal preparations and acupoint stimulation for breast cancer and should be reported according to PRISMA addressing potential bias and conflict of interest of included RCTs. Keywords: breast cancer, guideline, traditional Chinese medicine, scoping review, AMSTAR-2, ROBIS
