N2  - Purpose: The pathogenesis of myocardial infarction (MI) is associated with oxidative stress and neurohormonal overdrive, which intensify one another and exacerbate myocardial injury. Previous studies suggest that acupuncture has beneficial impact for various cardiovascular conditions, including MI. However, the mechanisms underlying the effects of acupuncture on MI and MIassociated oxidative stress and neurohormonal activities are unclear. Methods: MI induction was established in 20 Sprague- Dawley rats by left thoracotomy and surgical ligation of the left coronary artery. Thirty minutes post-MI, electroacupuncture (EA) was administered to 10 randomly selected infarcted rats at bilateral PC6 for 20 minutes at 1mA and 2/15Hz. Four hours post-MI, serum and myocardial tissue samples were collected for analysis. ELISA was used to assess lipid peroxidation (MDA), activity of pro-oxidant enzyme (NOX), and levels of nonenzymatic antioxidants (vitamin C and E). Neurohormonal activities were assessed by RT-qPCR to determine the relative mRNA expressions of ß1-adrenergic receptors (ß1-AR) and Angiotensin II Type 1 Receptor (AT1R) and by ELISA to measure serum levels of angiotensin II and aldosterone. Results: Infarcted rats treated withEAshowed decreased levels of oxidative stress compared to the nontreated infarcted rats: MDA (MI:8.32-0.67nmol/L vs.MI+EA: 2.21-0.31nmol/L; p<0.05) and NOX (MI: 25.28-3.63U/mL vs. MI+EA: 16.67-2.50U/mL; p<0.05). EA also increased levels of antioxidants: vitamin C (MI: 101.97-11.85nmol/L vs. MI+EA: 236.30-17.99nmol/L; p<0.05) and vitamin E (MI: 27.04-4.45nmol/L vs. MI+EA: 109.83- 12.10nmol/L; p<0.05). Neurohormonal activitiesweremodulated by EA: ß1-AR (MI: 2.53-0.28 vs. MI+EA: 1.60-0.32; p <0.05), AT1R (MI: 1.75-0.18 vs. MI+EA: 1.29-0.19; p<0.05), angiotensin II (MI: 242.96-12.94ng/L vs. MI+EA: 216.04-9.92ng/L; p<0.05), and aldosterone (MI: 335.17-32.80ng/L vs. MI+EA: 102.91-18.58ng/L; p<0.05). Conclusion: This study demonstrates that EA is associated with attenuated levels of oxidative stress and neurohormonal activities in the post-infarcted heart. Further studies are needed to better understand the efficacy and underlying mechanisms of acupuncture for MI.
