N2  - Statement of Purpose: The purpose of this study is twofold. First, to assess the adjunct role of Transcutaneous Electrical Acupoint Stimulation (TEAS) in reducing pain and rate of opioid administration in intra-operative tibia/fibula fractures. Secondly, to assess the role of TEAS using the validated Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) system and Quality of Life (EuroQol) score. Methods: This study will include orthopedic trauma patients admitted in the hospital trauma unit between the age groups of 18–65 years old. This will be a randomized controlled prospective study comparing isolated Tibia/Fibula fractures that undergo open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF). The pain control in these post-operative patients will be randomized to standard of care versus standard of care + TEAS. One group will receive standard of care (control group) and the other group will receive both TEAS with standard of care (treatment group). Randomization will be done by providing standard of care (opioid medication) to patients who arrive to the trauma unit on even days of the month versus standard of care + TEAS on odd days of the month. Patient consent will be performed prior to the operation when the patient is admitted to the trauma bay. Expected Results: Intraoperative TEAS could be considered an important component of the multimodal Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) approach. ERAS is a protocolized scientific pathway applied to enhance the recovery phase and increase outcomes after surgery (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6515715/). In addition to measuring pain control and recovery, this approach is designed to measure patient satisfaction with the EuroQol questionnaire score. Conclusion: Utilization of general anesthesia with intraoperative Transcutaneous Electrical Acupoint Stimulation (TEAS) may contribute to fewer complications, a reduction of cost, less postoperative pain, quicker return to work and normal function, and increased patient satisfaction.
