N2  - Introduction Thoracic epidural anesthesia (TEA) is a challenging procedure with a high failure rate (24-32%) nationwide. Even though simulation technology has gained importance in training residents, the purchasing and maintenance costs are prohibitive for many resident training programs. Previously we had demonstrated the feasibility of developing a custom-made low-cost three dimensionally printed simulator model obtained from patient CT scan data suitable for training residents in TEA. This is an ongoing study in which we determine the efficacy of the low-cost TEA simulator in pointof-care training for residents to perform a successful TEA. Materials and methods (NA for case report) We performed a randomized control study after obtaining waiver of consent from the Institutional Review Board. So far 7 residents undergoing thoracic and regional anesthesia training have completed the study. The study group (4 residents) underwent two 45-minute sessions of training before performing a thoracic epidural on patients in the operating room. Each training session consists of a 15-minute lecture on the anatomy of thoracic spine aided with a three-dimensionally printed thoracic spine model followed by a 30-minute hands-on training session with the TEA simulator where the residents were encouraged to perform a minimum of 4 epidurals. The control group (3 residents) underwent the traditional methods of observing TEA in the operating room and learning from books and videos about the procedure. Residents from both groups performed TEAs on patients in the operating room under the supervision of an attending anesthesiologist. The data was collected on the number of attempts taken for placing a successful TEA, whether any assistance from the attending physician was required and ability of the resident to identify thoracic structures using ultrasound and any rescue epidural placement in the recovery area. Results/Case report So far 7 residents have completed the study. We found that the residents who underwent training with the simulator required less time and decreased number of attempts to perform a successful TEA while the residents in the control group took longer time and more number of attempts for a successful TEA placement. The control group also required more assistance from the supervising anesthesiologist compared to the trained group. Moreover, the control group found it difficult and took longer time to identify the ultrasound anatomy and landmarks of the thoracic spine compared to the trained group. No rescue blocks were required in either groups. Discussion This study demonstrates the possibility of using a low-cost custom-made three dimensionally printed simulator for successfully training residents prior to performing a clinically challenging procedure. In cases of challenging anatomy residents can also be trained in patient-specific spine models.
