N2  - Background: Ample evidence suggested that 'fasttrack' (FT) program accelerates postoperative recovery after colorectal surgery. Our recent study also demonstrated that electroacupuncture (EA) can enhance recovery after laparoscopic colorectal surgery. This randomized noninferiority trial aimed to compare the efficacy of EA and FT program in reducing the duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay after laparoscopic colorectal surgery. Methods: Between 01/2014 and 03/2016, 164 patients undergoing elective laparoscopic colorectal surgery without conversion were randomized to receive either EA or FT program (82 per group). The primary outcome was time to defecation. Secondary outcomes were hospital stay, morbidity, and overall cost. Results: The demographic data of the two groups were comparable. The mean time to defecation in the EA and FT groups was 79 hrs and 72.9 hrs (difference = 6.1 hrs; 95%CI, -5.2 hrs to 17.5 hrs), respectively (P=0.286). Noninferiority was demonstrated as the upper limit of 95%CI for the difference was within the prespecified noninferiority margin of 24 hrs. There was a trend towards shorter mean length of hospital stay in the EA group (5.8d vs 6.8d, P=0.119). The overall 30-day morbidity in the EA and FT groups was similar (13.4% vs 22.0%, P=0.152). The total direct cost was lower in the EA group than in the FT group (HK$118,496 vs HK$132,642, P=0.049). Conclusions: EA is noninferior to FT program in reducing the duration of postoperative ileus after laparoscopic colorectal surgery. EA may be the preferred perioperative therapy for laparoscopic colorectal surgery because it is simpler to implement, less labor intensive, and less expensive than FT program.
