N2  - Background/Aims: Electroacupuncture (EA) at ST36 has been commonly used to treat gastrointestinal motility disorders; however the neuropathway of such an EA effect was unclear. The aim of this study was to prove the hypothesis that the ameliorating effect of EA on gastric dysmotility is mediated via the somatic-spinal-vagal efferent pathway. Methods: Rats were chronically implanted with serosal electrodes in the stomach for the measurement of gastric slow waves (GSW). Glucagon was used to induce slow wave dysrhythmia and dysmotility. Rats in the first experiment (N=8) were studied in five randomized sessions: control, glucacon (0.5 mg/kg), glucagon+EA, glucagon+EA+lidocaine applied at ST36, glucagon+EA+atropine. In the second experiment, spinalization at T7 level (N=5) or shamspinalization (N=5) was performed. In the third experiment, subdiaphragmatic bilateral truncal vagal nerve were severed in 8 rats. Autonomic functions were assessed by the spectral analysis of heart rate variability derived from the ECG. Results: 1) EA at ST36 normalized glucagon-induced GI dysmotility. Glucagon reduced the percentage of normal GSW (62±4% vs. 86±3%, P=0.03) and the impairment was completely normalized with EA at ST36. 2) The ameliorating effects of EA on glucagon-induced gastric slow wave dysrhythmia were completely abolished by application of lidocaine at ST36 (86±3% with EA vs. 67±6% with EA+lidocaine. P=0.01) or atropine (86±3% with EA vs. 43±8% with EA+Atropine, P<0.001). The bilateral vagotomy completely blocked the ameliorating effect of EA at ST36 on glucagoninduced slow wave dysrhythmia (86±3% with EA vs. 49±5% with EA+vagotomy, P=0.06) 3) Spinalization at T7 blocked the effect of EA on gastric slow waves. Compared with EA+sham-spinalization, spinalization+EA showed a reduced percentage of normal GSW (92±4%, 62±5% vs. P=0.04). 4) EA enhanced vagal activity (0.70±0.03 with EA vs.0.40±0.06 without EA, P<0.001); this increase was however blocked by lidocaine, spinalization or vagotomty. Conclusion: The ameliorated effects of EA at ST36 on glucagon-induced gastric dysrhythmia was attributed to enhanced vagal efferent activity mediated via the somatic nerve-spinal cord-vagus efferent-cholinergic pathway.
