N2  - Introduction: Deep brain stimulation in the subthalamic nucleus is common now to treat all symptoms of Parkinson's disease. Usually this kind of surgery was done under local anesthesia to evaluate the benefit of the surgery on rigidity, tremor and akinesia and to verify that there is no side effect. Drugs that can modify rigidity are not indicated. So during the surgery, morphinic drugs cannot be used because of decreasing the rigidity. To decrease the pain and the discomfort of the surgery, we used electro-acupuncture. Methods: Ten patients were done under local anesthesia and electroacupuncture. Patients were prepared the day before surgery with a video of the surgery and explanations of the electro-acupuncture technique, and one electro-acupuncture was done to test the sensibility and the tolerance of the patient. The day of the surgery, the patient was placed on the surgery table, and the electroacupuncture began before the surgeon put the frame on the head of the patient. Evaluation of VAS was done before the surgery, and at every painful phase of surgery and evaluation of the anxiety score. Results: Results were good, with decreased of pain on VAS of more than 50% compared to control subjects; anxiety was decreased too, and patients' satisfaction was evaluated, with 80% of patients reporting that pain VAS was about 2/10. They also had a decrease of anxiety score. Conclusions: Electro-acupuncture can help patients to decrease pain and surgical discomfort.
