N2  - Introduction & Objective: Although percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL) is a minimally invasive procedure for treatment of kidney stones, post-operative pain management remains a challenge. The objective of this study is to evaluate the efficacy of preoperative electro-acupuncture for post-operative pain relief in subjects undergoing PCNL. Materials and Methods: We conducted a double-blinded randomized, sham controlled pilot study to compare patients who received pre-operative true electro-acupuncture (group 1), preoperative sham-electro-acupuncture (group 2) and patients who did not receive any electro-acupuncture (group 3). All investigators, surgeons, anesthesiologists, nurses, and subjects were blinded, except for the acupuncturist. PCNL was performed according to standard protocol, but no intra-operative nerve block was administered. Post-operative pain was assessed by visual analog scale (VAS) immediately after the procedure (time 0) and over the subsequent 4 hours post-operatively and on postoperative day 1. Additionally, we recorded narcotic usage, incidence of post-operative nausea and vomiting and length of hospital stay. Results: A total of 30 patients have been enrolled in this study. There were 11 patients in group 1 and 11 patients in group 2 and 8 patients in group 3.MeanVAS score immediately post-operatively and 4 hrs post op was lower true electro-acupuncture group as compared to other groups. (Table 1) All 30 patients enrolled required narcotic usage. Average morphine miliequivalents used by true electro-acupuncture groupwas significantly less than the other groups. (Table 1) Conclusions: Our pilot data demonstrated that VAS scores were lower in the immediate postoperative period for patients who (Table presented) received true acupuncture. All subjects required the use of narcotics. Patients who received true electro-acupuncture required reduced amounts of narcotics compared as to patients who did not receive acupuncture as calculated by morphine miliequivalents. Further patient recruitment is in progress.
