N2  - Purpose: Postoperative ileus remains a significant problem after colorectal surgery that adversely influences patients' recovery and prolongs hospital stay. Our previous study demonstrated that electroacupuncture (EA) at Zusanli, Sanyinjiao, Hegu, and Zhigou reduces the duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay after laparoscopic resection of colonic and upper rectal cancer. This study aimed to evaluate the efficacy of EA in reducing the duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay after laparoscopic surgery for mid/low rectal cancer. (Table presented) Methods: Thirty consecutive patients who underwent laparoscopic surgery for mid/low rectal cancer without the need for conversion between May 2013 and May 2014 were prospectively recruited. All patients underwent one session of EA daily from postoperative days 1-4-The acupoints Zusanli, Sanyinjiao, Hegu, and Zhigou were used. They were compared with a matched historic cohort of 60 patients who underwent laparoscopic surgery for mid/low rectal cancer without EA between 2010 and 2012. The primary outcome was time to defecation. Secondary outcomes included postoperative analgesic requirement and length of hospital stay. Results: The two groups were comparable with respect to demographic data, tumor staging, proportion receiving neoadjuvant therapy, and types of surgery. Twenty three percent of patients in each group underwent abdominoperineal resection. Patients who received EA had significantly shorter time to first flatus (2.0 ± 1.0 vs. 2.7 ± 1.5 days; P = 0.039) and defecation (2.3 ± 1.4 vs. 3.3 ± 2.4 days; P = 0.036) than the control group. There was a trend toward earlier resumption of full diet in the EA group (4.1 ± 3.1 vs. 5.8 ± 5.0 days; P = 0.086). The median length of hospital stay was significantly shorter in the EA group than in the control group (6 [range, 4-29] vs. 9 [range, 4-110] days; P <0.001). Patients who received EA required significantly less postoperative analgesia; the postoperative pain scores on day 1 (3.3 ± 1.8 vs. 4.7 ± 2.3; P = 0.021) and day 2 (2.5 ± 1.7 vs. 3.7 ± 1.7; P = 0.021) also were significantly lower in the EA group. Postoperative complication rates were similar between the two groups. Three patients (10%) in the EA group and 8 patients (13.3%) in the control group developed prolonged ileus requiring parenteral nutritional support (P = 0.746). Conclusions: This prospective cohort study with matched historic controls suggests that EA can reduce the duration of postoperative ileus, length of hospital stay, and analgesic requirement, compared with no acupuncture, after laparoscopic surgery for mid and low rectal cancer. These results may provide the basis for planning a larger randomized controlled trial.
