N2  - Post-operative ileus (POI) increases healthcare costs due to extended hospitalization and reduces the postoperative quality of life after abdominal surgery. However, the therapy for POI is limited. The aim of this study was to investigate the effects and mechanisms of the electroacupuncture (EA) at ST36 and PC6 on gastrointestinal motility in a rodent model of POI. Methods: Rats were randomly divided into control group (CG, n=7), sham-treatment group (SG, n=8), and EA group (EG, n=7). For CG, under anesthesia, three abdominal electrodes were placed for the measurement of the electrocardiogram (ECG). Under laparotomy, two pairs of electrodes were implanted on the gastric antrum and mid- jejunum serosa, respectively. For SG/EG, after the same surgery of the CG, intestinal manipulation in open air by hands was performed for 15 min to induce POI. EA for EG was performed at ST36 and PC6 for 60 min using following parameters: PC6: train of on time of 0.1 s, off time of 0.4 s, 0.5 ms, 100Hz and 1 mA; ST36: on time of 2 s, off time of 3 s, 25Hz 0.5 ms and 4 mA. .Gastric emptying (GE) and small intestinal transit (SIT) were evaluated by the phenol red test. The gastric and intestinal slow waves were measured from the internal serosal electrodes. The sympathovagal balance (LF/HF) was assessed by the spectral analysis of the heart rate variability signal derived from the ECG. Results: 1) EA improved both GE and SIT in POI rats. Intestinal manipulation delayed GE that was normalized by EA (CG: 67.9±18.7%,SG: 36.8±14.9%,EG: 61.7±14.5%; CG vs SG: p<0.01, SG vs EG : p<0.05). Similarly, intestinal manipulation delayed SIT and EA normalized POI-induced delay in SIT (CG: 5.34±1.28, SG: 3.08±1.02, EG: 5.42±0.95; CG vs SG: p<0.01, SG vs EG: p<0.01. 2) Surgery reduced gastric slow wave frequency and intestinal manipulation did not reduce it any further. EA increased surgery-induced decrease in gastric slow wave frequency. The frequency (post-operative time 0-60 min) was 4.57±0.42 cycles/min (cpm) in CG, 4.69±0.45 cpm in SG and 5.54±0.40 cpm in EG; EG vs CG/SG: p<0.05). Similarly, surgery also reduced frequency of intestinal slow waves (reduced further by intestinal manipulatin) that was increased with EA. The frequency of intestinal slow waves (post-operative time 0-60 min) was 31.3±4.53 cpm in CG, 28.6 ±3.38 cpm in SG and 33.8±3.09 cpm in EG (EG vs SG: p<0.05). .3) Intestinal manipulation substantially increased sympathovagal balance and this was normalized by EA. LF/HF (post-operative time 150-180 min) was 1.05±0.36 in CG, increased to 2.62±2.07 in SG and normalized to 1.06±0.53 in EG (EG/CG vs SG: p<0.05). The vagal activity was not altered by EA. Conclusions: Intestinal manipulation induces POI; EA improves gastrointestinal transit as well as gastrointestinal slow waves by suppressing sympathetic activity.
