N2  - Aims: Transcutaneous electrical stimulation for the treatments of faecal incontinence (FI) has gained popularity as they are non-invasive and can be applied at home. However recent randomised controlled trial has shown inferior efficacy when compared to the percutaneous method. Acupuncture has been used in Traditional Chinese Medicine for the treatment of faecal incontinence. This study explores the efficacy of transcutaneous electro-stimulation on Acupoints for the treatment of faecal incontinence. Method: A pilot single centre study was carried out on 10 patients with FI. Demographic data and past medical history were recorded. Acu-TENS was applied to nine acupuncture points for the treatment of FI. Treatment includes twice weekly 30 min session for a total of 8 weeks. Assessment of FI via Cleveland Clinic Florida Faecal Incontinence Severity Score and the Faecal Incontinence Quality of Life Scale (FIQL) (Traditional Chinese version) questionnaires were carried out at baseline, 4, 8 and 16 weeks intervals. Results: 8 females and 2 male with a mean age of 59.6 years and mean BMI of 25.6 kg/m2 were enrolled. Following 8-week treatment, a 50% reduction in the Faecal incontinence severity score was achieved in 80% of patients. Episodes of incontinence and the ability of defer defecation for longer periods were also observed. None of the successfully treated patients reported recurrence of symptoms after 16 weeks. Conclusion: Treatment of Acu-TENS for faecal incontinence is feasible and safe. Further study is required to compare its efficacy to other transcutaneous electrical stimulation treatment of FI.
