N2  - Objective: To assess the effectiveness of acupuncture for the treatment of chronic nonspecific neck pain. Design: We reviewed articles found in the databases of MEDLINE from the beginning to May 2011 and analyzed randomized controlled trials. These articles were evaluated critically according to the Jadad scale. The search strategy used was based on the PICO structure (The initial “Patient,” “Intervention,” “Control”, “Outcome”) and the search syntax: (myofascial neck pain) OR neck pain AND (acupuncture therapy). The exclusion criteria for articles were: Jadad <3, fracture, previous surgery, radiculopathy, neck pain resulting from “whiplash”, arthritis, dystonia, headache. Setting: Clinical research center. Main Outcome Measures: Pain reduction (VAS: visual analogue scale). Results: We found 317 articles and 95 filtered as therapy/narrow. Seven randomized controlled trials were included, all with higher level of evidence (Jadad A) with a total of 11078 patients. Acupuncture reduces chronic neck pain uncomplicated with more than 3 months duration from 41.9 (32.7-51.0) (P<.001) to 70% (P<.001). When performed in five sessions maintained for 3 weeks for 20 to 30 minutes and stimulated manually every 5 minutes, acupuncture is better than massage (P=.0052) to reduce neck pain both in the short-term, immediately and 1 week after treatment (Decrease Pain-VAS %= 62.2 (28.5) with P<.001) and in the long term at 6 months post treatment (P=.014). Main acupuncture points used in the selected trials are: GB20, GB21, RL3, LI4, GB34 and UB10, UB60, LIv3, NT5. Significant improvement in pain scores are achieved when patients choose to receive acupuncture (P=.001). There is evidence that the association with auricular acupuncture (P<.001) and electroacupuncture significantly improves chronic neck pain, with a higher cost-benefit ratios (P<.001). Conclusions: Acupuncture is effective to reduce chronic neck pain in the short and long term.
