N2  - Purpose of this study: Pruritis is a sensation that provokes the desire or reflex to scratch. It is a common symptom that may be associated with a specific dermatologic condition or a systemic disease. The cause can be multifactorial or due to a single underlying disorder. Treatment is often ineffective, and as such, pruritus may be quite debilitating in some patients [1]. Acupuncture has been used in traditional Chinese medicine for over 2,500 years. It has recently started to gain popular support and nowadays, it is increasingly practiced in Western countries [2]. Furthermore, electroacupuncture (EA) is electrical stimulation through the acupuncture needles with short current pulses at different frequencies. It was suggested that various endogenous opioids such as β endorphin, enkephalin, and dynorphin are involved in electroacupuncture [3]. This working hypothesis about the role of endogenous opioids in acupuncture action has been widely accepted. The aims of the present study are to investigate whether electroacupuncture affects compulsive scratching in mice elicited by administrations (s.c; behind the neck) of: (1) 5′-guanidinonaltrindole (GNTI), a kappa opioid antagonist, (2) serotonin, an inflammatory mediator and (3) compound 48/80, a common pruritogen which rapidly releases of inflammatory mediators like histamine from mast cells located in connective tissue and skin. Methods: ICR mice were acclimated individually in rectangular observation boxes for at least 2 hours. Electroacupuncture was performed at LI4 (Hegu) and LI11 (Quchi) acupoints with the frequency of 2 or 100 Hz in mice under isoflurane anesthesia for 20 minutes. At 20 minutes after electroacupuncture, pruritogens were administered midline behind the neck of each mouse. In order to investigate whether pruritogens provoke c-fos expression and electroacupuncture inhibits these effects, c-fos immunoreac-tivity was determined. Animals were fixed with paraformaldehyde and the cervical spinal cords were removed and postfixed in paraformaldehyde solution overnight at 4 °C. Twelve cervical sections from each animal were randomly selected and the number of c-fos positive nuclei was counted. Cervical spinal cord (C5-C7) sections (30 μm) c-fos positive nuclei were observed under a light microscope and counted at 40× magnification. Summary of results: We found that electroacupuncture at both 2 Hz and 100 Hz attenuated serotonin (100nmol/site)-induced scratching. Electroacupuncture at 100 Hz attenuated compound 48/80 (0.1 (&g/kg)-induced scratching, whereas electroacupuncture at 2 Hz did not affect compound 48/80 induced scratching. Electroacupuncture at 2 Hz attenuated GNTI (0.3 mg/kg and 0.6mg/kg; s.c.)-induced scratching, whereas electroacupuncture at 100 Hz did not affect GNTI-induced scratching induced scratching. Moreover, EA at 2 Hz exerted at LI4 and LI11 decreased the number of c-fos positive nuclei on the lateral side of the superficial layer of the dorsal horn of the cervical spinal cord induced by pruritogens (Student's t-test, p < 0.05). Conclusions: It is concluded that electroacupuncture attenuated scratching behavior induced by serotonin, compound 48/80 and GNTI in mice.
