N2  - Aim: The study aimed to investigate the efficacy of electroacupuncture (EA) in reducing the duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay after laparoscopic colorectal surgery. Method: Between October 2008 and October 2010, 165 patients undergoing elective laparoscopic colorectal surgery were randomized to receive either EA, sham acupuncture (SA) or no acupuncture (control) (55 per group). Patients randomized to acupuncture groups underwent one session of acupuncture (to acupoints Zusanli, Sanyinjiao, Hegu, and Zhigou) daily from postoperative day 1 to 4. The primary outcome was time to defaecation. Results: The demographic data were similar between the groups. The EA group had significantly shorter time to defaecation (85.9 vs 122.1 hours, P < 0.001) and duration of hospital stay (6.5 vs 8.5 days, P = 0.01). The time to defaecation of the EA group was also shorter than that of the SA group (85.9 vs 107.5 hours, P = 0.007). Multivariate analysis revealed that absence of complications and EA were independent predictors of shorter duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay. Conclusion: This study suggests that EA is more effective than no acupuncture and SA in stimulating early return of bowel function. The use of EA is an independent predictor of shorter duration of postoperative ileus and hospital stay after laparoscopic colorectal surgery.
