N2  - Introduction: Treatment for chronic chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV) is often challenging. Since acupuncture on PC6 and ST36 acupoints is known to be effective in treating nausea and vomiting, we proposed a novel method of chronic implantation of electroacupuncture (EA) for the treatment of chronic CINV. Our aims were to: (1) establish a rodent model of chronic emesis, and (2) explore EA effects on kaolin intake using different stimulation parameters. Methods: Adult male Sprague-Dawley® rats were adapted to be single-housed in wire bottom cages with access to food, kaolin and water from 6:00PM-10:00AM daily. Rats were randomized to receive intraperitoneal injection with either saline (n = 3) or cisplatin (6-10mg Kg-1). Daily body weight, food, water and kaolin intakes were observed for 30 days. All rats underwent EA surgery for the placement of two pairs of electrodes at bilateral PC6 and ST36 acupoints. Following recovery, rats underwent 3 days of 1-3 h adaptation in Boll-man's cages. While restrained, rats received either Sham EA (electrodes connected but no stimulation was given) or EA (on bilateral PC6 and ST36) for two successive days, each. The frequency of EA was either 10 or 20 Hz, and its duration lasted for 1 or 3 h. Pulse width was maintained at 0.6 ms and its amplitude was from (0.4-2.0 mA). Following sham EA or EA, rats were placed in the wire bottom cages with access to food, kaolin and water. Average kaolin intake was observed for the following 24 h for after sham EA or EA for two consecutive days. Results: 1) Cisplatin treatment increased kaolin intake exponentially over 30 days: 0.01 ± 0.0; 0.19 ± 0.1; 1.70 ± 0.5; 2.88 ± 0.5 and 3.73 ± 0.5 for pre-treatment day 1 and post treatment days 1, 10, 20 and 30, respectively (P £ 0.01 for each vs pre-treatment intake). Whereas, saline-treated rats hardly ate any kaolin: intake varied from 0.00-0.06g at all times. 2) EA with 10Hz frequency was effective in reducing kaolin intake when given for 3 h, not 1 h; while EA with 20Hz frequency was effective when given for 1 or 3 h (see table). Conclusion: Chronic EA is effective in reducing pica in a rodent model of CINV. Further studies are warranted to explore the mechanism of action through which EA exerts its effect.
