N2  - Purpose: Pharmacology, physical exercise and surgery have been the mainstays of treatment for vestibular disorders. Acupuncture stimulation is reportedly effective in the management of dizziness but acupuncture is invasive and associated with adverse effects. This study investigates the effect of a non-invasive modality, the application of transcutaneous electrical nervous stimulation over acupuncture points (Acu-TENS), on balance control after a standardised vestibular challenge in normal healthy subjects. The relationship between Acu-TENS and autonomic nervous system modulation was also studied. Relevance: Physiotherapists often encounter patients with vestibular dysfunction in clinical practice, such as vestibular paresis and benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV). Exercises which emphasise postural stability have been the most common intervention adopted by physiotherapists to improve these circumstances. While there have been significant changes in the use of vestibular exercises over the past 5 years, mechanisms for vestibular compensation are still poorly understood and the efficacy of vestibular exercises as a specific treatment have been controversial. This study explored the role of Acu-TENS as a possible adjunctive therapy for people with a vestibular disorder. Participants: 25 (16 males) young subjects with normal health. Methods: Subjects were randomized to receive either Acu- TENS or Placebo-TENS as the first intervention protocol. The Acu-TENS protocol required TENS to be applied bilaterally to PC6 and SJ21 for 45 minutes prior to a 1 minute rotation at 240°/second on a rotational chair. Immediately after rotation, the total sway path, mean sway velocity and peak amplitude were recorded using a force platform. The Placebo-TENS protocol was identical to the Acu-TENS protocol except that there was no electrical output from theTENS device. Autonomic nervous system modulation was assessed using spectral analysis of heart rate variability. Analysis: Paired-t-tests were used to compare the variables of interest between the two interventions. Results: The total sway path and peak amplitude recorded after Acu-TENS were less than that after Placebo-TENS. The between-group differences were: total sway path 9.6±3.6 cm (p = 0.015), antero-posterior velocity (degree) 1.35±0.6 (p = 0.03), medial-lateral velocity 0.9±0.4 (p = 0.023) and peak amplitude 0.01±0.01 (p = 0.039). There was no significant modulation of the autonomic nervous system. Conclusions: The role of Acu-TENS in the management of vestibular dysfunction warrants further investigation. Implications: Results of this study suggest that Acu-TENS may be usefully employed to improve balance in people with vestibular disorders. Clinical application of Acu-TENS in patients with vestibular dysfunction warrants further investigation.
