N2  - Complimentary and Alterative Medicine (CAM) has been defined by the National Center for Cancer for Complimentary and Alterative Medicine (NCCAM) as “ a group of diverse medical health care systems, practices, and products that are not presently considered to be a part of conventional medicine”, and encompassed various forms of medicine such as dietary supplements, megadose vitamins, herbal preparations, special teas, acupuncture, massage therapy, magnet therapy, spiritual healing, and meditation. Japan is located in the Far East, and, historically, Oriental (Chinese) medicine, including herbal remedies and acupuncture, has been familiar to its residents. In 2003, we conducted a cohort study to clarify the precise picture of the prevalence of CAM use in Japanese patients with localized prostate cancer (Ref ). This study demonstrated that 20% of the patients had once used or had been using some types of CAM, and the most common types of CAM used were herbal medicine and vitamins. Following the above study, we planed to conduct a prospective open-label study to investigate the efficacy and safety of some types of medical mushroom, including extracts from the Agaricus Blazei Murill (ABM) mushroom, which is the most prevalent types of CAM used by Japanese cancer patients. Patients with non-metastatic prostate cancer, who had experienced a biochemical recurrence after radical prostatectomy or curative radiotherapy were eligible for this study. Patients chose one from two types of medical mushroom in their preference, and received the mushroom for 6 months. The primary endpoint was the effects of the mushrooms on the serum PSA levels of patients, and the secondary endpoints were 1) the safety profiles of the mushroom and 2) the effect on testosterone levels of the patients. In this lecture, the results of this prospective study will be presented, and I would like to show how to effectively use the CAM in present day management of the patients with prostate cancer.
