N2  - Introduction:- The burn injury is a universal problem. It is most serious and devastating form of trauma that man sustains. Millions of people all over the world are hospitalized each year for the treatment of burns and thousands of them die. Objective:- The main purpose of study was to evaluate the multiple effects of the prognostic factors on he outcome of extensive burn victims. Material and methods:- This prospective study was carried out on 50 patients who sustained extensive thermal burns (i.e. burn that covers more than 10% of TEAS) as defined by Mastern, at a 40 bed surgical unit of Nishtar Hospital, Multan in a period of 18 months from June 1997 to November 1998. Patients of either sex above 15 years of age who sustained extensive thermal burns were included in this study. Results:- Of the total 50 patients, 29 (58%) were male and 21 (42%) were female. Out of 50 patients, 35 (70%) patients were below the age of 30 years, whereas remaining 15 (30%) were above the age of 30 years. Of the total 50 patients, 45 (90%) had accidental burn, 2 (4%) were suicidal and 3 (6%) homicidal cases. In 30 (60%) patients, the burn was caused by dry heat (flames), in 5 (10%) patients burn was caused by electricity, in 15 (30%) patients the burn was caused by scalds. Of the total 50 patients, 20 (40%) patients had features of inhalation injury while in 30 (60%) patients there was no feature of respiratory involvement. Out of total of 50 patients, 5 (10%) patients had associated injuries. Among those 5 patients, 3 (6%) patients had features of long bone while 2 (4%) patients had associated head injury, while remaining 45% patients had no associated injury. Of the 50 patients, 26 (52%) died in the hospital. Among these, 4 (15.38%) patients died within 24 hours who had more than 74% burns and cause of death was shock. Conclusion:-Burn is a universal problem. No age is immune from burn injury but most of burn victims are below 30 years of age.
