N2  - Chronic pain is a common problem in spinal cord injured (SCI) patient. Different pharmaceutics, physical therapeutics and surgery can be tried in the management of pain. In this study 28 SCI patients between 18-36 years old were divided into two groups. Group I patients were administered amitryptiline 50 mg/daily in combination with carbamazepine 600 mg/daily. In group II, 4 Hz frequency electroacupuncture was applied for 10 days. Pain scores were recorded just before the application, 1, 6, 12, 24 and 48 hours and 7, 15, 30 and 60 days after the application in both group with 10 points numeric visual analogue scale. In both groups mean pain scores were significantly reduced at first hour which was most prominent in acupuncture group. This result was considered as placebo affect and/or patients' exaggeration in addition to exact therapeutic effect. In later measurements, in group I pain scores were reduced after 24 hour until 15th day and then reached a plateau. In group II, decrease in pain scores began in 12 hours and continued till 7th day and then reached a plateau. After discontinuation of electroacupuncture, pain score began to increase. There was no complication. In conclusion: we suggest that both therapeutic modalities are safe and effective in the management of chronic pain in SCI patient.
