N2  - The purpose of this study was to assess the results of 49 'isolated' tears of the supra-spinatus operated on 49 patients, 32 males and 17 females. The average age at operation was 54,2 years (35 to 72) and the dominant side was injured in 30 patients (12 opposite side and 5 unknown). At operation, 39 patients had an isolated tear of the supraspinatus and 10 an associated tear of the long head of the biceps (7 tendinitis, 1 dislocation and 2 ruptures). The size of the rupture was less than 2 cm2 in 21 cases, comprised between 2 and 5 cm2 in 27 and upper than 5 cm2 in one case. The surgical procedure was a Neviaser's technique in 4 cases, a single suture in 19, transosseous suture in 19 and a deltoid muscular flap in 7 cases (big retracted tear); in addition we performed 14 resections of the outer end of the clavicle and 4 tenodesis of the long head of the biceps. The results were assessed according to Constant's score on 46 patients (3 lost to follow-up); the average follow-up was 31,5 months (12 to 86). The average scores were: pain 12,3 points (3 to 15), range of motion 35,5 (14 to 40), daily living activity 17,4 (8 to 20), and strengh 11,4 ('healthy' side = 14,8 points). The total Constant's scores were: 75,7 per cent (actual score) and 88,3 per cent (weighted score). If we compare the results in terms of the surgical procedure the difference was not statistically significant although the transosseous suture was not so good: single suture 91,5 per cent, deltoid muscular flap 90,9 per cent, Neviaser's technique 87, and transosseus suture 79,1. Nevertheless the results are not so good (p = 0,01) if it is an industrial injury and if the post-operative acromio-humeral height is diminished. On the other hand, age, sex, duration of pain, occupation, tenodesis of the long head of the biceps and resection of the lateral end of the clavicle don't have any pejorative influence on the results.
